I was, of course, like most of us, a customer at one point.

21
00:01:30,800 --> 00:01:43,520
I was CIO of Dow Jones News Corp, uh, very early cloud customer, worked a lot with AWS and other folks on how to sort of take Dow Jones down that digital journey and adopt cloud, got to learn practices.

22
00:01:43,680 --> 00:01:48,880
It was a time of DevOps and containerization and leveraging managed services It was a great time.

23
00:01:48,960 --> 00:01:51,760
Now you're going to try to calculate my tenure and time.

24
00:01:52,080 --> 00:01:58,692
but about seven years ago, AWS asked me to join them and take what I learned and done and do it with many other customers.

25
00:01:59,118 --> 00:02:02,400
And more importantly, they got me on the leadership principles.

26
00:02:02,480 --> 00:02:03,600
We love our leadership principles.

27
00:02:03,680 --> 00:02:05,280
They're like, do you want to learn?

28
00:02:05,742 --> 00:02:07,120
Learn and be curious is a big one for us.

29
00:02:07,200 --> 00:02:11,360
Do you want to learn what all the most interesting companies in the world are doing and help them do it?

30
00:02:11,440 --> 00:02:12,900
So I became a solution architect.

31
00:02:13,262 --> 00:02:16,484
And that led me to the path where today I get to manage that function globally.

32
00:02:17,294 --> 00:02:17,840
Fantastic.

33
00:02:20,238 --> 00:02:22,880
So I lead our AI engineering team.

34
00:02:23,040 --> 00:02:26,320
It's a team spread across different countries and areas.

35
00:02:27,086 --> 00:02:36,080
the way we started was almost two years back, as AI was becoming more and more popular, Gen EA especially, and there was a lot of confusion.

36
00:02:37,360 --> 00:02:43,920
one confusion was, oh, Gen AI becoming more popular means developers are not going to have any work to do.

37
00:02:44,110 --> 00:02:48,292
Some of that was confusion among leaders, like what is our role, what we should do.

38
00:02:48,480 --> 00:02:55,360
So we thought as AWS we should provide clarity, and the clarity will come only if we have conviction on what works and what not.

39
00:02:55,520 --> 00:02:57,520
So the team was rooted in the foundation.

40
00:02:57,760 --> 00:03:04,400
The foundation of the team was learn and understand what works and what not when it comes to building products faster, better.

41
00:03:04,686 --> 00:03:06,000
So that was the genesis of the team.

42
00:03:06,080 --> 00:03:09,520
One thing led to other, and we have several offerings like AIDLC.

43
00:03:09,710 --> 00:03:12,320
We also help customers build product development lifecycle.

44
00:03:12,526 --> 00:03:15,492
We also help them set up tiger teams across the teams.

45
00:03:15,760 --> 00:03:20,480
we do a lot of work on autonomous sites, like what can be done autonomously, and also token economics.

46
00:03:20,640 --> 00:03:24,000
So I'm happy to talk throughout the interview which we have today.

47
00:03:24,560 --> 00:03:38,720
but my journey started almost 18 and a half years back when I was fresh out of college living with some roommates, and we used to go every weekend to different hotels where a lot of these companies used to come and offer free food.

48
00:03:39,600 --> 00:03:42,640
and then some interviews which happen, and there's a chance to join the company.

49
00:03:42,720 --> 00:03:47,440
So the primary motivation was, oh, it's happening in a fancy hotel, and I'll get some good food?

50
00:03:47,600 --> 00:03:49,220
I didn't know what Amazon is that time.

51
00:03:49,840 --> 00:03:54,852
And of course, my interview went well and got selected.

52
00:03:55,280 --> 00:03:59,840
And for first 10 years I worked across several teams as an engineer.

53
00:04:00,160 --> 00:04:06,480
One was Kindle Team, which was my first team, then Prime Team, Amazon machine learning team.

54
00:04:06,720 --> 00:04:12,160
Having been through several Amazon retail and other teams, I learned that there's a lot happening.

55
00:04:12,240 --> 00:04:16,640
There's a lot of engineering principles, but I was having a age to see what do startups do?

56
00:04:16,720 --> 00:04:18,240
Like how do they do load balancing?

57
00:04:18,320 --> 00:04:20,800
And because a lot of this work was done for us by central teams.

58
00:04:20,880 --> 00:04:22,400
So I wanted to see the whole picture.

59
00:04:22,670 --> 00:04:24,160
What decisions are CTOs making?

60
00:04:24,240 --> 00:04:33,760
So I moved to Bay Area, worked with several startups, and that was very exciting to see how a company of five people can scale so fast and become a billion dollar company.

61
00:04:33,840 --> 00:04:36,160
And now there's a lot of them are like trillion dollar companies.

62
00:04:36,240 --> 00:04:39,680
So very, very happy to see the progression of some of the customers.

63
00:04:40,384 --> 00:04:45,344
but personally I thought I should learn to become a leader as well.

64
00:04:45,454 --> 00:04:51,344
So I moved to London, uh led a team in Europe for startups, and then I led a team in India for solution architects.

65
00:04:51,918 --> 00:05:01,316
So I feel very happy that Amazon gave me an opportunity to try different things and feel very fulfilled that I get to contribute and learn every day.

66
00:05:01,966 --> 00:05:02,704
What a great story.

67
00:05:02,784 --> 00:05:06,052
And I agree, I think the fastest way to a developer's heart is through their stomach.

68
00:05:06,384 --> 00:05:08,944
So I'm aligned on the free food angle.

69
00:05:09,424 --> 00:05:10,308
Free food, I love it.

70
00:05:10,544 --> 00:05:11,024
Noted.

71
00:05:11,342 --> 00:05:11,584
Thank you.

72
00:05:11,664 --> 00:05:13,664
Yeah, we offer free food and people will show up.

73
00:05:13,904 --> 00:05:18,704
Well, the topic of today's episode is the AI-driven development lifecycle, AI DLC.

74
00:05:19,054 --> 00:05:23,024
Um, Sean, could you tell me a little bit, starting at the ground level, what is AI DLC?

75
00:05:23,438 --> 00:05:28,704
You know, to talk about what it is, you have to talk about the problem statement or the opportunity statement.

76
00:05:28,864 --> 00:05:37,284
And it's been three plus years where partners, vendors, startups, everyone's been talking about building with AI.

77
00:05:37,742 --> 00:05:40,100
And they were eye-popping numbers in the beginning.

78
00:05:40,464 --> 00:05:43,904
You can say 60, 70, 80% of engineering time.

79
00:05:44,238 --> 00:05:44,464
Great.

80
00:05:44,878 --> 00:05:45,504
People love that.

81
00:05:45,806 --> 00:05:47,140
Build faster, build better.

82
00:05:47,694 --> 00:05:51,140
But the reality didn't necessarily map to the theory.

83
00:05:51,438 --> 00:05:58,692
And what we overwhelmingly heard for customers who went and bought this two years ago was the value wasn't what they expected.

84
00:05:59,024 --> 00:06:10,532
I was with the CFO of a very large commerce company this week, and he told me they're spending $30 million on tokens for engineering purposes, and he thinks the measurable value is seven or eight million.

85
00:06:10,926 --> 00:06:12,384
It's not a great statement, right?

86
00:06:12,686 --> 00:06:16,384
And I took him through AIDLC, and I'll take you through the same mental model.

87
00:06:16,784 --> 00:06:25,060
when engineers are working, especially on existing applications, you sometimes find they're spending less than an hour of their day actually writing code.

88
00:06:25,486 --> 00:06:26,544
Most engineers know this.

89
00:06:26,784 --> 00:06:31,664
Unless you have the privilege of building your own unique thing, you're interacting with lots of people.

90
00:06:31,982 --> 00:06:37,664
You got requirements coming at you, business analysts, UI folks, finance asking you the effort.

91
00:06:37,744 --> 00:06:40,784
You got a scrum master setting time there is so much happening.

92
00:06:41,006 --> 00:06:44,784
Coordination tax, testing tax, translation tax.

93
00:06:45,390 --> 00:06:55,024
And we really wanted to build a methodology that was backed by software and best practices that would bring all of that together, that would drive real appreciable outcomes.

94
00:06:55,184 --> 00:07:01,504
So you could build things, not code, but outcomes fast, know how to measure them.

95
00:07:01,584 --> 00:07:03,264
And we've seen incredible results.

96
00:07:03,520 --> 00:07:08,384
Anupam will tell you about a lot of the numbers, but when I talk to customers about it, they are blown away.

97
00:07:08,464 --> 00:07:12,676
And it's not just the outcomes, it's that their teams are working together as one team.

98
00:07:13,774 --> 00:07:14,224
Fantastic.

99
00:07:14,860 --> 00:07:16,864
Anupam, I'd love to dig a little deeper as well.

100
00:07:16,944 --> 00:07:19,664
What would AI DLC look like for a typical developer?

101
00:07:19,744 --> 00:07:21,572
How would they interface with it at the start?

102
00:07:22,414 --> 00:07:26,704
Yeah, I think it's good to think about what developers do on a daily basis to start with.

103
00:07:26,864 --> 00:07:34,544
I mean, generally a developer, people think that they are writing code most of the time, but some of our teams have done analysis.

104
00:07:34,624 --> 00:07:41,092
Like there's one team internally, AWS, uh, one of the AWS teams, they shared that they analyzed everything which developers are doing.

105
00:07:41,358 --> 00:07:45,664
About 50% of them was coordination and looking at requirements, clarification.

106
00:07:45,902 --> 00:07:49,104
30% of that was coding, and 20% was everything else.

107
00:07:49,344 --> 00:08:03,584
So the thinking is a developer is spending, assuming let's say this 30% is the time which they spend, even if you double that productivity there, as a team or as an end-to-end system, you're only 15% better.

108
00:08:04,686 --> 00:08:12,864
so if you look at what a developer does today, they are spending time negotiating with product managers, understanding what needs to be done, what are the other functions doing.

109
00:08:12,944 --> 00:08:16,704
With the ADLC, there's a common platform given to everybody.

110
00:08:17,504 --> 00:08:25,824
because one of the most common anecdotes I hear is uh engineering leader telling that some of my developers, which are best with AI, they are 10 times faster than others.

111
00:08:25,966 --> 00:08:33,424
But there are others, like 80% of the org, which is still learning and still not able to deliver, even though they have access to exactly same tools, same environment.

112
00:08:33,678 --> 00:08:39,664
So ADLC brings that common platform or a way of working which brings everybody together.

113
00:08:39,824 --> 00:08:40,900
Now, how does it happen?

114
00:08:41,424 --> 00:08:42,784
there are two, three parts to it.

115
00:08:42,944 --> 00:08:45,824
One is having a clear understanding of what we are building.

116
00:08:45,984 --> 00:08:53,824
The second part is once you know what it is, how do you build the context which we can use across the other cycles so that the outcomes are better?

117
00:08:53,904 --> 00:08:55,984
There are two or three big learnings we had in this part.

118
00:08:56,144 --> 00:09:04,196
One is the more complex the problem is, which you give to AI, the more chances it is going to go it wrong, get it wrong or hallucinate, as we call it.

119
00:09:04,558 --> 00:09:08,452
So we do a process where the complex problem is broken into smaller parts.

120
00:09:09,024 --> 00:09:09,904
that is one part of it.

121
00:09:10,064 --> 00:09:11,044
The second part of it is.

122
00:09:11,822 --> 00:09:15,424
When you have multiple teams, small teams work much better than bigger teams.

123
00:09:15,504 --> 00:09:24,304
So rather than a developer working and eight of them staring at the same screen or eight of them doing this in their own isolation, we believe that 2-3 team is a pretty good sized team.

124
00:09:24,544 --> 00:09:31,504
In Amazon, we talk about two-pizza teams, but we are seeing smaller than two-pizza teams is what a lot of customers are seeing better outcomes.

125
00:09:31,664 --> 00:09:35,908
In fact, we have our own learnings there in terms of small teams delivering much better outcomes.

126
00:09:36,398 --> 00:09:38,784
And the third thing is context engineering.

127
00:09:38,864 --> 00:09:46,064
This is another piece where most companies have developers deciding and building context on their own, on their own machine.

128
00:09:46,144 --> 00:09:56,548
But we are seeing once you have common processes in which you understand what should be known to AI versus what should be known to human, and how do you make it accessible?

129
00:09:57,184 --> 00:10:03,744
And I think it makes it much easier for developers to I think a lot of people call it compound engineering.

130
00:10:03,824 --> 00:10:11,972
What it means is if a developer does a good work, that compounds over time, because every time an engineer does a good thing, it compounds at a company level.

131
00:10:12,544 --> 00:10:14,304
so it keeps getting better and better.

132
00:10:14,384 --> 00:10:25,904
So if you're doing a task today, and let's say that task takes two days, it's very likely if you do AI DLC, the time it takes will keep reducing as time goes because you will have better context.

133
00:10:26,064 --> 00:10:34,704
AI, you can learn what decisions human is taking, which of these judgment type decisions can be codified so that the human effort goes lower and lower as time progresses.

134
00:10:34,864 --> 00:10:34,944
Yeah.

135
00:10:35,184 --> 00:10:35,904
That resonates with me.

136
00:10:36,064 --> 00:10:43,204
One of the things that I've noticed leading AI DLC engagements with my own customers is one of the things I really think people maybe underestimate about it.

137
00:10:43,694 --> 00:10:48,580
It requires you to be very specific in what your use case is and what your success criteria is.

138
00:10:49,344 --> 00:10:54,864
that first step of being very, very specific in terms of here's what we want to build, but here are all the edge cases we need to capture.

139
00:10:55,264 --> 00:11:03,104
I think it forces developers in a positive way to be very specific with what they're looking to build before they start having again, agenda harnesses.

140
00:11:03,424 --> 00:11:09,984
It's a very good insight because a lot of times this information is split across 10 to 20 people depending on the size of the company.

141
00:11:10,064 --> 00:11:22,084
Like some of this information is with product leaders, some of this is with business and GTM leaders, some of this is security people who know specific constraints, some of this is compliance people who see we are not allowed to store data this way, that way.

142
00:11:22,318 --> 00:11:29,984
So we do something where we bring people together for two to three hours, where we get all these things and constraints out of their head into AI's context.

143
00:11:30,222 --> 00:11:38,704
So it's much easier for people to settle on what we are doing and what we are not, rather than this information still being distributed across so many people.

144
00:11:39,024 --> 00:11:51,748
There's a very interesting element of a startup mentality here where if you think about companies that have gotten scaled and big, the developers and the engineers often get very disconnected from the business purpose of what they're building.

145
00:11:52,302 --> 00:11:54,784
And I don't mean the MBA jargon stuff.

146
00:11:54,894 --> 00:11:56,420
I mean like what's it actually do?

147
00:11:56,718 --> 00:11:58,020
How do users use it?

148
00:11:58,414 --> 00:12:06,704
And the more modular code got over the years, the more you're building a back-end module or a specific piece part or something else, you got you got sort of forgot about it.

149
00:12:06,784 --> 00:12:08,800
And that's what led to this big coordination tax.

150
00:12:08,944 --> 00:12:12,624
Someone else had to actually be thinking about how it plugged in, how it flowed, did it work right?

151
00:12:12,910 --> 00:12:14,384
The testing got onerous.

152
00:12:14,544 --> 00:12:25,184
And so we're seeing an interesting pattern where developers who have a command of their business are exponentially more valuable and they can have that bigger and better output.

153
00:12:25,518 --> 00:12:34,180
So, especially when you get into like sort of frontier development on the agentic side, and you're building an agent with a small team, maybe it's two or three people, maybe it's one or two people.

154
00:12:34,606 --> 00:12:39,424
If they understand the whys and the what's, maybe the compliance team helped explain it to them.

155
00:12:39,504 --> 00:12:44,804
And now it's sort of in their heads and they understand the spirit behind it, they can build so much faster and more effectively with less iteration.

156
00:12:45,518 --> 00:12:48,804
Yeah, I mean we call it blurring of the boundaries between roles.

157
00:12:49,038 --> 00:12:50,660
So a lot of blurring is happening.

158
00:12:50,912 --> 00:12:55,792
Product managers traditionally used to write a PID or BRD product requirement doc.

159
00:12:56,142 --> 00:12:57,792
Now they are creating demos on their own.

160
00:12:57,952 --> 00:12:59,312
They are creating prototypes on their own.

161
00:12:59,712 --> 00:13:00,832
No technical person is needed.

162
00:13:01,006 --> 00:13:10,672
In fact, some of the smarter companies, let's call them frontier companies for simplicity, they are already having product managers working on creating three or four prototypes for the same problem.

163
00:13:10,990 --> 00:13:18,672
They're going to customers and validating which one will convert better and which one will be helping them prove the hypothesis better.

164
00:13:18,752 --> 00:13:32,832
And they're going with much stronger conviction and a very measurable hypothesis, which allows them to be much better as a company because every company talks about fail fast, but practically it's very hard to fail fast when your engineering takes six to nine months to do something.

165
00:13:32,992 --> 00:13:34,272
People move on to other things.

166
00:13:34,432 --> 00:13:40,272
But now you can build things in a few weeks and then you can see whether the hypothesis we had is true, true or not.

167
00:13:40,526 --> 00:13:45,956
It's funny, for those of us who have been rooted in cloud for a long time, you know, there's some familiar patterns emerging here.

168
00:13:46,352 --> 00:13:50,992
Like one would be the idea that you don't have to scope everything out forever.

169
00:13:51,278 --> 00:14:08,112
And if I go back to, you know, a decade ago when cloud was really hitting its popular peaks, I'd have to go explain to CFOs all the time, you don't have to plan your CapEx allocation for three months and then land the hardware and then buy the software and then build the product and realize the product wasn't right and throw all your investments.

170
00:14:08,272 --> 00:14:10,192
Well, this has now come to the engineering world.

171
00:14:10,382 --> 00:14:15,072
What you just described, Anupam, you prototype it out versus saying, what is this solution?

172
00:14:15,232 --> 00:14:26,352
You you sort of drop that analysis paralysis time on how to build it, or maybe that iterative time and I'll do version one and then a year ago do version two, and then maybe version three is what lands with the customer three years or two years in.

173
00:14:26,894 --> 00:14:38,832
I do think it's all about fail fast, but I was also unpleasantly surprised a year, two years ago when I started talking to folks about fail fast and they'd have this enterprise folks allergic reaction.

174
00:14:38,912 --> 00:14:40,272
They're like, isn't failure bad?

175
00:14:40,432 --> 00:14:42,032
And I'm like, no, no, no.

176
00:14:42,318 --> 00:14:48,804
You want to identify if the idea is right as early in the life cycle as possible and then move on to the next thing.

177
00:14:49,134 --> 00:14:52,032
And that process you described is one of the ways to get there.

178
00:14:52,206 --> 00:14:52,432
Yeah.

179
00:14:52,512 --> 00:14:57,552
I mean, a lot of people are calling it product development lifecycle, which is yes, you know how to deliver fast.

180
00:14:57,646 --> 00:15:01,092
A lot of companies are working on delivering things fast, but how do you know what to deliver?

181
00:15:01,614 --> 00:15:09,952
If a company delivers, let's say, 10 features on a given month and they change it to 30 features in a given month, how do we know it's better to deliver 30?

182
00:15:10,032 --> 00:15:14,672
Because you can argue and say maybe these 30 features are confusing our people.

183
00:15:14,832 --> 00:15:17,668
Like just because it's cheaper and faster doesn't mean you have to do it.

184
00:15:18,032 --> 00:15:30,352
So, I'm seeing smarter companies are doing a lot more work on discovery side as well, which is, let's also do the right analysis to see what signals we are getting from customers, analyze them in a scalable way.

185
00:15:30,542 --> 00:15:38,912
look at the competitive signals, look at ideation in a much more scalable way, and checking the hypothesis and seeing whether it's true or not.

186
00:15:39,072 --> 00:15:44,192
So, yeah, it's changing the way, trajectory of the company in a lot of cases, but also a lot more ownership.

187
00:15:44,272 --> 00:15:46,660
In Amazon, we take a lot of pride in talking about ownership.

188
00:15:46,926 --> 00:15:53,792
I see the level of ownership changes because the anti-pattern which I see in some engineering teams is mostly it happens with junior engineers.

189
00:15:53,872 --> 00:15:57,508
They're thinking that my job is to do this task, and as long as this task is done, I'm done.

190
00:15:58,032 --> 00:16:00,752
And they were more like I'm a task person.

191
00:16:00,832 --> 00:16:03,460
Like somebody give me a task and I do my task and go away.

192
00:16:03,952 --> 00:16:08,388
But once they elevate themselves to see why we are doing it, what does it mean for a company?

193
00:16:08,672 --> 00:16:14,192
It drives a lot better outcomes because they know if we launch this feature, we are going to have our conversion increase by 10%.

194
00:16:14,512 --> 00:16:17,952
Or if we launch this feature we're going to get uh 20% more revenue.

195
00:16:18,112 --> 00:16:21,252
So I think it's it's uh making people much more of an owner.

196
00:16:21,552 --> 00:16:28,752
Well, you talk about value in software development, and the story I opened the conversation with around the CFO I spoke to.

197
00:16:28,992 --> 00:16:37,712
Like it's not about building the features faster and better or more of them, it's about building the right features and having them actually hit the outcomes that you're trying to measure.

198
00:16:37,792 --> 00:16:43,152
And so we talk a lot to customers about how to bring ROI into the engineering conversation.

199
00:16:43,342 --> 00:16:44,832
Like, why am I building this?

200
00:16:45,070 --> 00:16:49,712
Even if it's something critical, like a security feature, you can still measure return on investment for that.

201
00:16:49,792 --> 00:16:50,992
Like you have to do it.

202
00:16:51,232 --> 00:16:59,044
And I think what's hard there is when engineers and developers are super disconnected from the business, the ROI stuff doesn't make any sense to them.

203
00:16:59,662 --> 00:17:04,192
But once they have even a basic conversational level understanding of it, it starts to resonate.

204
00:17:04,272 --> 00:17:07,312
And then they're not driven to a task, they're driven to an outcome.

205
00:17:07,472 --> 00:17:07,632
Yeah.

206
00:17:07,918 --> 00:17:13,072
And so if they build the feature and the code doesn't break, but the outcome is not achieved, they're dissatisfied.

207
00:17:13,232 --> 00:17:13,792
And that's great.

208
00:17:13,966 --> 00:17:17,872
It's great for the business, it's great for that person because most of us don't want to do drudgery, right?

209
00:17:18,032 --> 00:17:21,472
Most of us aren't inspired to be like, I'm building this thing someone wrote a spec on.

210
00:17:21,552 --> 00:17:27,312
She'll tell you the most depressing developer story, and it's a personal one, involves a relative.

211
00:17:27,552 --> 00:17:34,032
I can't uh dox my relative, so I won't say who it is, but their first job, they were working on what I thought was really cool.

212
00:17:34,112 --> 00:17:35,152
This is forever ago.

213
00:17:35,472 --> 00:17:39,204
they're working on a software for a part of a ship.

214
00:17:40,238 --> 00:17:41,184
And I was like, oh, that's really cool.

215
00:17:41,264 --> 00:17:42,224
So it's going to control the ship.

216
00:17:42,318 --> 00:17:43,024
It's going to do this thing.

217
00:17:43,104 --> 00:17:49,664
They're like, well, it controls the movement of this one little widget and this one little part of moving the widget.

218
00:17:49,744 --> 00:17:55,664
So if I finish this in nine months, then someone else does their work and someone else does their work, this little thing will be able to turn.

219
00:17:55,720 --> 00:17:57,584
And I'm like, that sounds very unexciting.

220
00:17:58,064 --> 00:18:01,664
Oh and he was more progressive than most.

221
00:18:01,774 --> 00:18:04,144
Like he at least understood where it fit in that ecosystem.

222
00:18:04,334 --> 00:18:07,104
Now you can build things where you can actually see what it does afterward.

223
00:18:07,504 --> 00:18:09,424
And that's like way more inspiring.

224
00:18:10,030 --> 00:18:15,904
What have some of the developer reactions been in AIDLC engagements that you've seen as well with that increase in pace, increase in scope?

225
00:18:16,110 --> 00:18:17,080
What's the sentiment been?

226
00:18:17,294 --> 00:18:21,184
Well, I'm going to start out, but Audit Palm's done, I think, 10 times as many as I've been in.

227
00:18:21,424 --> 00:18:28,784
The one thing that I see is people go through a little bit of a uh the classic, like a moment of disillusionment before the excitement.

228
00:18:29,038 --> 00:18:32,544
Like the first day, they're like, why am I talking about specs?

229
00:18:32,704 --> 00:18:34,704
Why am I in this long conversation?

230
00:18:34,864 --> 00:18:36,772
I'm ready to start coding.

231
00:18:37,230 --> 00:18:38,884
Why is this really useful?

232
00:18:39,310 --> 00:18:49,572
And then when they see things coming out that resonate with their partners, the partner might be the UI person, the product manager, the analyst, the compliance person, they get excited.

233
00:18:50,350 --> 00:18:54,948
And so I think you get this really fast move to excitement and happiness.

234
00:18:55,502 --> 00:19:00,464
I know I talked a lot about ROI, but it is true builders who are excited are going to build faster and better.

235
00:19:00,942 --> 00:19:01,984
I don't know, Anupam, you can go deep.

236
00:19:02,144 --> 00:19:06,384
Yeah, I mean, I think we have worked with more than 1,000 customers just this year.

237
00:19:06,544 --> 00:19:18,916
So it's uh it's a wealth of information we have in terms of how different companies of different sizes, some of them are Fortune 100 companies, some of them are startups, some of them are regulated customers, some in defense industry, some are in finance industry.

238
00:19:19,278 --> 00:19:21,444
And across the board, there are a few common things.

239
00:19:21,870 --> 00:19:27,744
One, I think there are some developers which are front runners, they they can navigate the ambiguity no matter what.

240
00:19:27,886 --> 00:19:28,784
This is an ambiguous area.

241
00:19:28,864 --> 00:19:31,264
We just have to understand there's no one right solution.

242
00:19:31,424 --> 00:19:33,104
Right solution keeps evolving with time.

243
00:19:33,264 --> 00:19:37,664
So people have to be accustomed to keep learning.

244
00:19:37,744 --> 00:19:40,900
Like that, this is the actually, if people ask me, like, what is the right skill for this?

245
00:19:41,166 --> 00:19:47,236
The right skill is dealing with ambiguity because what you built one year back, may probably there's a much better way to do it.

246
00:19:47,598 --> 00:20:02,436
The second one I've seen is with developers who are at the frontier already, like those who are already deciding a better way of working across the company, they start thinking about taking the whole company along rather than thinking that I have to become the best one.

247
00:20:02,702 --> 00:20:05,184
And I think that is an exponential benefit for the whole company.

248
00:20:05,344 --> 00:20:07,980
So some companies are creating separate teams.

249
00:20:08,064 --> 00:20:10,532
some are calling them tiger teams in North America.

250
00:20:10,862 --> 00:20:13,824
In Japan, I've seen some companies are calling them samurai teams.

251
00:20:13,904 --> 00:20:16,064
So they're giving local flavor to it.

252
00:20:16,224 --> 00:20:27,344
But a team which has some of the most passionate people about what AI can do for their company, and they take this as a challenge that I will not stop until I make the whole company go much faster.

253
00:20:27,504 --> 00:20:31,984
Faster here is not the only thing because sometimes faster with low quality means nothing.

254
00:20:32,464 --> 00:20:35,812
If you build something in half the time, but you have double the bugs, what's the point?

255
00:20:36,110 --> 00:20:37,584
So faster goes with quality.

256
00:20:37,710 --> 00:20:38,944
So you need to have quality metrics.

257
00:20:39,024 --> 00:20:44,944
Some of the teams are measuring, for example, on the faster side, they will measure things like deployments per builder.

258
00:20:45,024 --> 00:20:52,224
So let's say we have 1,000 developers, you can say how many deployments we were doing pre-AI, how many deployments we are doing after AI, and then you can have a number there.

259
00:20:52,494 --> 00:20:53,264
Same with quality.

260
00:20:53,390 --> 00:20:58,224
So for example, in Amazon we still call quality defect as page.

261
00:20:58,384 --> 00:21:02,144
So we have a team called on-call team, which is supporting the production software.

262
00:21:02,464 --> 00:21:07,824
a percentage of development team is assigned as on-call, and we call them pagers, like paging people.

263
00:21:08,144 --> 00:21:11,780
so we used to page them earlier, but now they have phone apps and all that.

264
00:21:12,174 --> 00:21:16,100
So we see how many times we are paging an average on-call.

265
00:21:16,304 --> 00:21:23,556
So, for example, we had a team which had 15 pages per on-call coming on an average, and after AI it went to almost half.

266
00:21:24,046 --> 00:21:26,624
So I think these are like metrics which people should anchor on.

267
00:21:26,784 --> 00:21:33,504
And developers are creating the discipline where they're anchoring on metrics which can quantify certain things.

268
00:21:33,774 --> 00:21:37,024
Some of them are even creating blended score across all these measures.

269
00:21:37,104 --> 00:21:40,704
So you can say this team has score of 87, this team has score of 55.

270
00:21:40,784 --> 00:21:45,344
So you can see what does a team with score 55 needs to do to get to get to 87, right?

271
00:21:45,424 --> 00:21:50,224
That builds the culture where the whole company keeps moving forward because there's no right or wrong answer right now.

272
00:21:50,304 --> 00:21:56,612
I mean, you probably will anchor on things which are which are showing results, and as time progresses, those things will keep improving.

273
00:21:57,360 --> 00:21:58,240
Makes a ton of sense.

274
00:21:58,400 --> 00:22:06,960
And I would love to dive a little deeper on what separates customers pursuing AI DLC that succeed and those that maybe have a bit more friction in the process.

275
00:22:07,342 --> 00:22:12,640
Are there any common threads you've noticed on what customers do well that really helps to enable their success with AI DLC?

276
00:22:13,614 --> 00:22:18,720
Look, I will start with one foundational concept.

277
00:22:19,054 --> 00:22:28,400
When you run that first workshop or session, it's not just bringing people who are interested in learning and want to understand it, but it's bringing a diverse set of folks.

278
00:22:28,560 --> 00:22:30,788
You don't just bring engineers into the room, right?

279
00:22:31,360 --> 00:22:37,120
Whether it's product or UI or analyst or compliance, you need all the folks involved in getting to the outcome.

280
00:22:37,390 --> 00:22:38,160
And that's part two.

281
00:22:38,480 --> 00:22:40,740
You have a defined opportunity to solve.

282
00:22:41,294 --> 00:22:42,800
Problem, outcome, something else.

283
00:22:42,960 --> 00:22:47,280
You know, I think we've all learned over the years that education only takes you so far.

284
00:22:47,502 --> 00:22:52,292
When I have a class, when I come and you know, show you something, when I bring you my PowerPoint, it's practitioning.

285
00:22:52,590 --> 00:22:54,240
People learn through practitioning.

286
00:22:54,480 --> 00:22:56,960
And you only have the time to practition on real problems.

287
00:22:57,040 --> 00:22:59,680
It's not like you really want to just go through some demo idea.

288
00:22:59,950 --> 00:23:06,404
So if you bring that full team in place, you bring a set of people who want to learn and you bring a business problem to solve.

289
00:23:06,926 --> 00:23:07,280
Boom.

290
00:23:07,566 --> 00:23:10,560
You're going to see it, you're going to set a timeline, you're going to solve that problem.

291
00:23:10,640 --> 00:23:11,520
You're going to know it's real.

292
00:23:11,600 --> 00:23:12,560
You're going to see how it works.

293
00:23:12,640 --> 00:23:14,000
You're going to demonstrate it to others.

294
00:23:14,080 --> 00:23:15,120
You're going to scale it out.

295
00:23:15,374 --> 00:23:16,388
The magic's going to happen.

296
00:23:17,230 --> 00:23:22,212
Yeah, I think that's the foundation to doing a successful AIDLC engagement.

297
00:23:23,200 --> 00:23:27,120
I think what we have learned is this is both top-down and bottom-up problem.

298
00:23:27,280 --> 00:23:32,720
So, what I mean by that is a leader has to feel the need that my company needs to move fast.

299
00:23:32,800 --> 00:23:40,320
I meet several CTOs who say, I've been given the task of making our teams 30, 40% more productive and I'm struggling with it.

300
00:23:40,400 --> 00:23:41,092
What should I do?

301
00:23:41,422 --> 00:23:45,924
So that need has to be there, and the people in the team should believe that we have to do something about it.

302
00:23:46,702 --> 00:23:53,220
Bottom-up is important as well, where a leader cannot go to all developers and say, do this because I heard somebody say that.

303
00:23:54,000 --> 00:23:55,040
They have to have conviction.

304
00:23:55,120 --> 00:24:05,440
And the conviction comes by you having a baseline of what we do today, a measurable baseline, and then having a very quantifiable way to say how good we are compared to baseline.

305
00:24:05,600 --> 00:24:19,120
So for that to happen, you need to have practitioners who have a current estimate of what they are doing, and then a tangible way to prove that what you were supposed to do or what you were about to do got done in one-fourth of the time with higher quality.

306
00:24:19,200 --> 00:24:20,160
So why would you not do it?

307
00:24:20,270 --> 00:24:26,084
So it builds conviction at the bottom line or bottom level, and that can start spreading across several teams.

308
00:24:26,350 --> 00:24:31,360
But a leader has to be a sponsor because otherwise people feel like I don't know if I'm doing the right thing.

309
00:24:31,520 --> 00:24:33,520
I don't know if this is important for our company.

310
00:24:33,680 --> 00:24:35,172
So both go hand in hand.

311
00:24:35,440 --> 00:24:38,960
And I also feel the more real the use case is, the better it is.

312
00:24:39,040 --> 00:24:51,280
There are so many toy examples of AI, like, oh, I built this beautiful website which has these funny, funny animations, or or I could build this game, like tic-tac-toe being a popular one I see in like five minutes.

313
00:24:51,440 --> 00:24:54,720
A lot of developers or a lot of teams can brush it off saying, So what?

314
00:24:54,800 --> 00:24:56,400
I'm not building tic-tac-toe every day.

315
00:24:56,814 --> 00:24:58,080
Like, why do I care?

316
00:24:58,320 --> 00:25:10,240
So I have seen generally people have much higher conviction when the task or the problems on which you're about to spend the time, and maybe I'll ask you, Sean, like say you have a busy calendar of things which you do.

317
00:25:10,320 --> 00:25:12,720
You have thousands of people which are in your team.

318
00:25:12,880 --> 00:25:20,880
If somebody tells you that, Sean, for everything you're doing next week, if that can be done in one day, it will bring a level of conviction where you'll say, why would I not do it?

319
00:25:21,070 --> 00:25:21,280
That's right.

320
00:25:21,520 --> 00:25:24,240
I have four extra days to do other things which I really want to do.

321
00:25:24,320 --> 00:25:29,280
So I think it builds that level of conviction when you see the problem which were which was really important for you.

322
00:25:29,454 --> 00:25:32,240
You were about to spend that time anyways, and that got done.

323
00:25:32,320 --> 00:25:38,400
And that conviction is just a starting point, in my view, where people think that this is really an investment area for us.

324
00:25:38,560 --> 00:25:41,188
Because on day one, day two, you're not going to figure out everything.

325
00:25:41,454 --> 00:25:48,560
But it gives you a direction where there's a motivation, there are mechanisms which you set up for going on that journey.

326
00:25:48,654 --> 00:25:52,420
This is a long journey, and and the journey, as we talked, is a continuous journey.

327
00:25:52,782 --> 00:25:54,800
Uh, but you have to be on journey, this journey.

328
00:25:54,960 --> 00:25:58,160
And in fact, I meet some customers who are in financial services.

329
00:25:58,240 --> 00:25:59,844
I met a hedge fund recently.

330
00:26:00,206 --> 00:26:04,400
They shared, we would rather disrupt ourselves before somebody else disrupts us.

331
00:26:04,718 --> 00:26:06,880
And this is a common mentality I'm seeing in several companies.

332
00:26:06,960 --> 00:26:12,960
They're realizing that code or a product is no longer the biggest mode.

333
00:26:13,040 --> 00:26:17,840
There are several other things, and they have to be moving fast and learn because they have two choices.

334
00:26:18,000 --> 00:26:21,604
One, they can wait until everybody figures out what AI does or not.

335
00:26:22,000 --> 00:26:26,756
And even if they wait for, let's say, one, two, three years, they'll still have the learning curve.

336
00:26:27,086 --> 00:26:30,160
But others are saying, yes, the journey is not straight line.

337
00:26:30,380 --> 00:26:31,360
We'll have ups and downs.

338
00:26:31,440 --> 00:26:37,280
We will learn a few things and we'll go on this journey, but we better be on this journey because we'll be relevant as time progresses.

339
00:26:37,520 --> 00:26:38,480
It's a funny moment.

340
00:26:38,574 --> 00:26:41,924
I think that sense of urgency that's driving folks to action.

341
00:26:42,350 --> 00:26:50,852
You know, there is a perception a couple of years ago that all of these coding tools and AI assistance for coding would be all about cost savings.

342
00:26:51,950 --> 00:26:54,096
And now there's an increasing realization.

343
00:26:54,176 --> 00:27:01,376
It's about velocity, building fast and building well and driving new outcomes for whoever your end customers are.

344
00:27:01,550 --> 00:27:04,736
It doesn't matter what type of company you are, you always have an end customer somewhere.

345
00:27:04,976 --> 00:27:05,536
Right.

346
00:27:05,856 --> 00:27:13,296
And I talk a lot about folks who actually wait for it to settle down, see which tool set wins, you know, go hire the perfect talent.

347
00:27:13,456 --> 00:27:15,332
I'm like, you probably have the perfect talent.

348
00:27:15,854 --> 00:27:21,156
And the tool sets are out there are already way more capable than what you can take advantage of.

349
00:27:21,518 --> 00:27:21,936
All of them.

350
00:27:22,016 --> 00:27:22,896
There's a stack of them.

351
00:27:23,056 --> 00:27:27,936
We think some are better suited for AI DLC type use cases than others, but they can all be made to work.

352
00:27:28,096 --> 00:27:38,020
So if you're not tool constrained and you actually have talent, how can you harness them to get to this faster velocity and outcomes before someone comes and disrupts you?

353
00:27:38,606 --> 00:27:40,676
And it's not a fear thing, it's an opportunity thing.

354
00:27:40,896 --> 00:27:45,572
Like you want to be that disruptor, but that's also a hard mental model for folks to get.

355
00:27:45,870 --> 00:27:54,148
People are rarely thinking about how to throw away the code they've spent years building and go and do something new, a new concept that leads to a rooted business outcome.

356
00:27:54,496 --> 00:28:00,896
And I think one of the things that's really resonated with my customers with AIDLC is the fact that it's tooling agnostic, it's language agnostic.

357
00:28:00,976 --> 00:28:04,496
You don't have to meet the needs and requirements of AI DLC.

358
00:28:04,576 --> 00:28:07,376
It meets you where you are as an engineering organization.

359
00:28:07,520 --> 00:28:10,336
How does that influence the conversation when it comes to AI DLC?

360
00:28:10,656 --> 00:28:10,896
It does.

361
00:28:10,976 --> 00:28:19,044
Look, what I find is while we are very tooling agnostic, most companies have not actually settle down on a tool that they're set with for life.

362
00:28:19,630 --> 00:28:31,204
They have something they've tried out, maybe they found success in a couple scenarios, but model changes and pricing changes, without going too technically deep, I'll give you some guardrails that the decision makers on tooling can think about.

363
00:28:31,936 --> 00:28:37,284
One, you know, you want to be careful that you're not pegged to a single model or model family.

364
00:28:37,838 --> 00:28:49,668
It's a challenging thing to say, but we are seeing so much innovation in the model space that if you can find tool chains that allow you to take advantage of that, you're probably going to come out ahead in the fullness of time.

365
00:28:50,030 --> 00:28:51,460
Not a short-term priority.

366
00:28:51,936 --> 00:28:53,936
just get a tool that works for your short term.

367
00:28:54,286 --> 00:29:01,056
But certainly in the medium and long term, you want to think about things like cost and scalability and supporting different types of outcomes.

368
00:29:01,216 --> 00:29:05,696
And then, second, we see an increasing focus on spec-driven development, right?

369
00:29:05,902 --> 00:29:06,816
Building out specs.

370
00:29:06,896 --> 00:29:12,576
And I had a fun conversation where we're talking about the fact that at Amazon, we have a great tool set we built out.

371
00:29:12,656 --> 00:29:13,296
It's called Kiro.

372
00:29:13,536 --> 00:29:14,576
We're super passionate about Kiro.

373
00:29:14,816 --> 00:29:16,416
We use it internally a ton.

374
00:29:16,686 --> 00:29:18,016
All of our essays use it.

375
00:29:18,176 --> 00:29:18,948
I use it.

376
00:29:19,936 --> 00:29:30,096
and you know, one of the things we've learned with it is when we built one of our early first backend systems in com using Kiro, we had these great outcomes.

377
00:29:30,256 --> 00:29:32,336
It was faster, it was quicker, less bugs.

378
00:29:32,416 --> 00:29:34,816
You know, I could tell you the stats and you'd be impressed.

379
00:29:35,214 --> 00:29:37,252
That wasn't what was the mental model shift.

380
00:29:37,518 --> 00:29:39,296
It was when we went to build v2.

381
00:29:39,758 --> 00:29:46,576
So you have a back-end application, you're building v2, you have all this stuff on your backlog, you have some bugs, you have some features.

382
00:29:46,958 --> 00:29:48,996
And we threw away 100% of the code.

383
00:29:49,870 --> 00:29:52,896
When I tell the CTO that, they were like, huh.

384
00:29:53,390 --> 00:29:54,576
So the code was bad.

385
00:29:54,990 --> 00:29:57,136
I'm like, no, no, no, the code wasn't bad.

386
00:29:57,296 --> 00:29:58,496
That wasn't what we created.

387
00:29:58,656 --> 00:29:59,696
We created the specs.

388
00:29:59,854 --> 00:30:02,256
We used Kiro to build the right specifications.

389
00:30:02,416 --> 00:30:06,276
And so when we updated the specifications, we rejanned all the modules and code.

390
00:30:06,960 --> 00:30:08,612
That We weren't living in the code.

391
00:30:09,262 --> 00:30:11,056
And that's such a mental model change.

392
00:30:11,438 --> 00:30:12,816
So Kiro is a great tool set.

393
00:30:12,976 --> 00:30:14,176
We support many others.

394
00:30:14,350 --> 00:30:19,056
The point of us uh sort of working with customers on AI DLC is to change how they work.

395
00:30:19,216 --> 00:30:22,416
We know it'll help them build, it'll help them build on AWS.

396
00:30:22,606 --> 00:30:28,656
We have all the right higher level services that engineers want to use to go and get those apps and agents and applications built.

397
00:30:28,736 --> 00:30:31,136
So that's sort of been our mental model from day one.

398
00:30:31,438 --> 00:30:33,216
And you mentioned V2 as well.

399
00:30:33,358 --> 00:30:35,936
Um, and Anupam, maybe this is a good question for you.

400
00:30:36,096 --> 00:30:39,776
Where do you see AI DLC going in the next, say, 12 to 18 months?

401
00:30:39,856 --> 00:30:40,836
What does the future hold?

402
00:30:42,190 --> 00:30:46,660
Actually, we are seeing a lot of shift towards work happening autonomously in some cases.

403
00:30:46,832 --> 00:30:49,712
Now it sounds very scary to a lot of people.

404
00:30:49,792 --> 00:30:50,992
How can it happen autonomously?

405
00:30:51,072 --> 00:30:56,324
And if it is happening autonomously, who is liable for that change and what is happening there?

406
00:30:57,312 --> 00:31:03,268
building on what uh Shaun shared, uh I mean, we while we have been using Kiro, we are also improving a lot of things.

407
00:31:03,472 --> 00:31:13,636
So we had several internal teams who created something called Meshclaw, and it ended up becoming Kiro Crew as a product which we launched.

408
00:31:13,792 --> 00:31:20,672
And that was being used by thousands of engineers to deliver features and products, but also autonomously solving several of the challenges.

409
00:31:20,832 --> 00:31:33,312
So we are seeing a similar shift happening across the board, where companies are classifying the type of problem they have and looking at the complexity, and based on that, they can decide what happens autonomously versus not.

410
00:31:33,472 --> 00:31:40,672
Now, autonomously is not a magic here because in theory you can solve almost every problem, throw it to AI, and AI will do something about it.

411
00:31:40,832 --> 00:31:43,392
But how do you build conviction that this is done in the right way?

412
00:31:43,632 --> 00:31:48,592
So the thinking which we have is autonomy is something which you earn, not grant.

413
00:31:48,878 --> 00:31:51,792
So you don't like suddenly say I want to be autonomous.

414
00:31:51,952 --> 00:31:53,632
For that, a lot of groundwork needs to be done.

415
00:31:53,872 --> 00:32:01,232
Groundwork involves what are the review, mechanisms you have what have you done in CICD space?

416
00:32:01,552 --> 00:32:05,792
what have you done in terms of maintaining high quality of the of the code?

417
00:32:06,030 --> 00:32:17,232
So there's a lot of groundwork which needs to be done for you to be successful, but I do feel a lot of companies are able to get about 30 to 40 percent of their operational work solved fully autonomously using AI.

418
00:32:17,312 --> 00:32:18,752
So that's one directional change.

419
00:32:18,926 --> 00:32:24,432
Another one we are seeing is context engineering, harness engineering, these are the terms which keep popping up.

420
00:32:24,672 --> 00:32:31,712
A lot of companies are the anti-pattern here is companies believe that I point AI to everything which I have and will find out.

421
00:32:32,014 --> 00:32:40,548
It doesn't work because one anti-pattern, for example, is you will have a lot of large code repositories, it has millions of lines of code and point AI to it.

422
00:32:40,782 --> 00:32:49,872
And we see that when you point AI to millions of lines of code, it has a recall issue where something is clearly specified, but it will ignore it even if you have specified it.

423
00:32:50,032 --> 00:32:57,392
So of course AI is non-deterministic by nature, but you cannot get great outcomes when the context is so big and you have pointed it to everything.

424
00:32:57,710 --> 00:33:01,152
So we're seeing a lot of engineering happening on how context can be maintained.

425
00:33:01,232 --> 00:33:07,044
So we have launched a feature called AWS Context, which is going to be out in hands of customers very soon.

426
00:33:07,792 --> 00:33:16,432
but on the parallel, we are also seeing a lot of work on how can we make context engineering much easier for companies.

427
00:33:16,592 --> 00:33:18,912
How can they do context management for code?

428
00:33:19,118 --> 00:33:24,964
How can they do context management for their information and some of the decisions which people are taking?

429
00:33:25,392 --> 00:33:29,572
So those are some of the high level trends which I'm seeing.

430
00:33:30,094 --> 00:33:30,432
Makes sense.

431
00:33:30,512 --> 00:33:33,232
And I'm excited to see what comes next for AI DLC as well.

432
00:33:33,358 --> 00:33:35,460
Um, I know there's a lot of uh exciting things in the works.

433
00:33:35,950 --> 00:33:39,632
How widespread are we seeing the adoption of open weight models with AI DLC today?

434
00:33:39,872 --> 00:33:50,564
You know, I think what you're seeing is customer realization that something we've been preaching, um, and we built our bedrock platform with this in mind, one model will not rule them all.

435
00:33:51,246 --> 00:33:55,872
And so having the right fit model for the outcome is so important.

436
00:33:56,032 --> 00:33:57,712
And there are a couple of barriers to that, right?

437
00:33:57,872 --> 00:33:58,672
One is complexity.

438
00:33:58,752 --> 00:34:00,032
There's a new model all the time.

439
00:34:00,430 --> 00:34:02,340
Fine, you got to keep your eyes on that.

440
00:34:02,798 --> 00:34:14,832
Second, uh, the platform, if you're directly calling a model provider, if you're calling their first party APIs, you usually get the latest features and the latest performance gains, but now you're stuck on sort of one path.

441
00:34:15,470 --> 00:34:22,608
And so customers who embrace bedrock have been seeing access to open weight models that we validate, that we've put in our own secure environment.

442
00:34:22,768 --> 00:34:28,608
You know, we have models like Kimi and GLM and Deep Seek and even some Nemotron models available.

443
00:34:28,768 --> 00:34:38,208
And customers can then bake off do I want the latest GPT or Grok or anthropic model, or do I want one of their open weight models?

444
00:34:38,368 --> 00:34:40,208
GPT has OSS models as well.

445
00:34:40,368 --> 00:34:41,248
Do I want Gemma?

446
00:34:41,648 --> 00:34:46,608
And for developers, having a harness that can do some of that selection for them.

447
00:34:46,768 --> 00:34:51,168
It's like a developer wants to pick a different model every time they're trying to run a different type of autonomous task.

448
00:34:51,328 --> 00:34:55,088
And so we've putting a lot of effort into Cura to help you make that model selection for you.

449
00:34:55,168 --> 00:34:59,008
So we're seeing usage go up, up, up, but it's not so much about open weight.

450
00:34:59,088 --> 00:35:03,248
It's about the choice of models, different price points, different performance characteristics.

451
00:35:03,328 --> 00:35:07,808
And, you know, we see the frontier companies releasing more and more model choices every week, which is awesome.

452
00:35:07,888 --> 00:35:09,568
And that opens up that opportunity even further.

453
00:35:09,934 --> 00:35:16,128
Sean, if there were any pieces of advice that you give to IT leaders looking to implement AI in their development lifecycle, what would you give?

454
00:35:16,430 --> 00:35:18,180
You know, I have a lot.

455
00:35:18,542 --> 00:35:21,604
So let me think of a couple juicy ones that we might enjoy.

456
00:35:21,902 --> 00:35:24,036
First, partner with line of business leaders.

457
00:35:24,558 --> 00:35:27,364
You know, I talked about being rooted in business outcomes.

458
00:35:27,918 --> 00:35:35,888
And if we continue to think about the technology teams as sort of a standalone function that's off in isolation, you get that same level of disconnect at a higher level.

459
00:35:36,142 --> 00:35:37,488
What am I prioritizing building?

460
00:35:37,648 --> 00:35:39,168
What am I prioritizing delivering?

461
00:35:39,248 --> 00:35:40,048
How does that work?

462
00:35:40,288 --> 00:35:46,756
So once you have that right partnership with your line of business leaders, don't focus on the glitz of the tools, focus on the outcomes.

463
00:35:47,214 --> 00:35:48,608
Then they're going to appreciate you.

464
00:35:48,768 --> 00:35:52,368
They're going to really be impressed by the things your team is doing.

465
00:35:52,848 --> 00:35:56,132
next up, look for the curious folks in your team.

466
00:35:56,686 --> 00:36:06,048
They're the ones who are going to lead the charge, the ones who want to try that next methodology, that next tool, the ones who want to learn about the business they're living in and operating in that they're part of.

467
00:36:06,288 --> 00:36:08,288
I think that will really accelerate it.

468
00:36:08,368 --> 00:36:17,968
And I think more and more, especially in sort of enterprises and ISVs and even startups, the 10x engineers, as we used to call them, are going to be the ones who know that business.

469
00:36:18,350 --> 00:36:20,644
And last but not least, look, check out AIDLC.

470
00:36:20,928 --> 00:36:22,368
I mean, we're pretty passionate about it.

471
00:36:22,448 --> 00:36:24,292
It's not a service that we charge for.

472
00:36:24,848 --> 00:36:29,508
we recommend you use it with our tool sets with Kiro and Kiro crew, but it works with many others.

473
00:36:29,806 --> 00:36:31,556
So there's a lot of opportunity to get going.

474
00:36:31,888 --> 00:36:35,488
And do not be stuck in analysis paralysis.

475
00:36:35,568 --> 00:36:36,288
Just try it out.

476
00:36:36,448 --> 00:36:37,248
Build something.

477
00:36:37,518 --> 00:36:40,128
Well, Sean, Anupam, it was great having you on the show.

478
00:36:40,208 --> 00:36:42,128
I really appreciate you taking the time.

479
00:36:42,608 --> 00:36:43,408
thank you very much.

480
00:36:43,648 --> 00:36:44,368
Thanks for having us.

481
00:36:44,608 --> 00:36:44,768
Thank you.

482
00:36:44,928 --> 00:36:45,808
That was fun.

